1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,574
On the morning of the third day, a Greek army of thousands picks up its shields and walks away.

2
00:00:06,074 --> 00:00:08,326
It leaves a few hundred men behind.

3
00:00:08,826 --> 00:00:11,104
Their king has ordered the rest to go.

4
00:00:11,454 --> 00:00:14,084
He stays, and so do the men who came with him.

5
00:00:14,584 --> 00:00:18,998
Behind them, high on a mountain, Persian soldiers have found a path nobody guarded.

6
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:21,855
By midday the pass will be surrounded.

7
00:00:22,355 --> 00:00:26,261
For two days, these Greeks have been the hardest thing in the world to move.

8
00:00:26,611 --> 00:00:28,863
By the end of the third, they are dead.

9
00:00:29,363 --> 00:00:33,491
So how does a small force stop the largest army anyone has ever seen?

10
00:00:33,991 --> 00:00:37,259
And how does it lose, after all that, to a goat track?

11
00:00:37,759 --> 00:00:41,495
Most people will tell you it was courage, and then it was a traitor.

12
00:00:41,845 --> 00:00:42,965
Both are true.

13
00:00:43,315 --> 00:00:45,112
Neither is the real answer.

14
00:00:45,612 --> 00:00:47,630
The real answer is geometry.

15
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,362
And a plan with exactly one weak point.

16
00:00:50,862 --> 00:00:52,490
Let's go back to the start.

17
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:55,463
It is the summer of 480 BC.

18
00:00:55,813 --> 00:00:59,029
Xerxes, king of Persia, is marching on Greece.

19
00:00:59,529 --> 00:01:03,162
His father tried this ten years earlier and lost at Marathon.

20
00:01:03,662 --> 00:01:07,242
Xerxes means to finish the job, and he has brought everything.

21
00:01:07,592 --> 00:01:11,980
Soldiers from every corner of an empire that stretches from Egypt to India.

22
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:13,730
A fleet to match.

23
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:18,715
A bridge of boats across the Hellespont, because he did not want to wait for a ferry.

24
00:01:19,215 --> 00:01:20,335
How many men?

25
00:01:20,685 --> 00:01:23,393
Herodotus says more than two and a half million.

26
00:01:23,893 --> 00:01:27,577
Herodotus was a wonderful storyteller and a poor accountant.

27
00:01:27,927 --> 00:01:32,537
Modern historians say somewhere between one hundred thousand and a few hundred thousand.

28
00:01:33,037 --> 00:01:36,773
Even the low end is the largest force the Greek world has ever faced.

29
00:01:37,273 --> 00:01:39,747
The Greeks cannot beat it on open ground.

30
00:01:40,097 --> 00:01:41,282
They know that.

31
00:01:41,782 --> 00:01:44,920
So they look for a place where open ground does not exist.
